1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,847
Your skates feel slower and looser on a cold morning, and there is a simple reason for it.

2
00:00:05,847 --> 00:00:14,033
In the next few minutes you will learn what cold does to your wheels, your bearings and your body, and exactly what to change.

3
00:00:14,033 --> 00:00:15,853
Three things happen at once.

4
00:00:15,853 --> 00:00:18,321
Cold urethane stiffens and grips less.

5
00:00:18,321 --> 00:00:22,350
Cold bearing oil thickens and drags for the first few minutes.

6
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:28,587
And a cold road often carries a thin film of dew that you cannot see until a wheel slides on it.

7
00:00:28,587 --> 00:00:31,121
None of that is a fault in your skates.

8
00:00:31,121 --> 00:00:33,525
It is physics, and you can manage it.

9
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:35,676
Start with the wheels.

10
00:00:35,676 --> 00:00:38,117
Urethane is sensitive to temperature.

11
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:42,404
Warm it, and it softens, flexes more under load and grips harder.

12
00:00:42,404 --> 00:00:48,407
On the left, the warm wheel squashes into a wider contact patch, so it holds the road well.

13
00:00:48,407 --> 00:00:50,716
Cool it, and the compound stiffens.

14
00:00:50,716 --> 00:00:57,906
On the right, the cold wheel flexes less, its contact patch shrinks, and it holds the surface with less bite.

15
00:00:57,906 --> 00:01:03,051
So a cold wheel behaves like a harder wheel than the grade printed on the box.

16
00:01:03,051 --> 00:01:07,603
That is why skaters wash out on the first corner of a winter session.

17
00:01:07,603 --> 00:01:10,637
Your technique did not go backwards overnight.

18
00:01:10,637 --> 00:01:16,046
The wheel is simply harder than the one you set your lean for yesterday afternoon.

19
00:01:16,746 --> 00:01:19,273
Timing matters a lot on a cold morning.

20
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:28,346
A wheel reaches its working temperature partly from the road surface and partly from the heat it makes as it flexes and recovers under load.

21
00:01:28,346 --> 00:01:31,715
At six in the morning, none of that heat exists yet.

22
00:01:31,715 --> 00:01:35,992
So your first laps are the least grippy laps of the whole session.

23
00:01:35,992 --> 00:01:41,436
They are also the laps when your muscles are coldest and your reactions are slowest.

24
00:01:41,436 --> 00:01:47,527
Put those two things together, and you have the moment when most early morning crashes happen.

25
00:01:47,527 --> 00:01:51,350
Treat those first laps as information, not as a time trial.

26
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:54,820
Bearing oil also changes with temperature.

27
00:01:54,820 --> 00:02:00,358
Cold oil is thicker, and thicker oil takes more energy as the balls roll through it.

28
00:02:00,358 --> 00:02:02,534
This timeline shows what happens.

29
00:02:02,534 --> 00:02:07,545
At minute zero, the oil is thick, the roll is short and the wheels are hard.

30
00:02:07,545 --> 00:02:11,436
As you skate, the oil warms and thins, and grip comes back.

31
00:02:11,436 --> 00:02:15,326
By around minute fifteen, roll and grip are back to normal.

32
00:02:15,326 --> 00:02:18,755
So do not judge a set of bearings on a cold morning.

33
00:02:18,755 --> 00:02:28,315
If you want to check whether a bearing is worn, do it indoors at room temperature, and listen for grit and roughness rather than timing the spin.

34
00:02:28,315 --> 00:02:34,052
And if your bearings still feel slow after the first ten minutes, that is not the cold.

35
00:02:34,052 --> 00:02:35,898
That is grit, water or wear.

36
00:02:36,598 --> 00:02:40,751
Do not try to fix cold drag by flooding the bearing with oil.

37
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:42,657
Heavy oils protect but drag.

38
00:02:42,657 --> 00:02:47,559
Very thin oils feel fast for ten minutes and then leave the bearing dry.

39
00:02:47,559 --> 00:02:51,985
A purpose made skate bearing oil sits between those two extremes.

40
00:02:51,985 --> 00:02:55,729
One drop per bearing is the whole dose, all year round.

41
00:02:55,729 --> 00:02:59,406
More than that collects dust and adds drag of its own.

42
00:02:59,406 --> 00:03:08,256
If your bearings really are slow once everything is warm, the fix is to clean them, dry them fully and put one fresh drop in each.

43
00:03:08,256 --> 00:03:12,001
The routine is the same for steel and ceramic bearings.

44
00:03:12,701 --> 00:03:17,613
In most of India, the bigger cold weather hazard is not the temperature.

45
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:18,910
It is the moisture.

46
00:03:18,910 --> 00:03:23,754
On a clear winter night, the road loses heat and water condenses on it.

47
00:03:23,754 --> 00:03:30,168
By first light, smooth tarmac can carry an invisible film of dew that behaves like light rain.

48
00:03:30,168 --> 00:03:35,626
Painted lines, tiled sections, metal covers and polished concrete are the worst.

49
00:03:35,626 --> 00:03:39,515
They stay damp long after the rest of the road looks dry.

50
00:03:39,515 --> 00:03:40,743
Shade is the trap.

51
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:46,679
A road that is dry in the sun can be wet under a flyover, beside a wall or under trees.

52
00:03:46,679 --> 00:03:50,841
So scout your first lap slowly and note where the shade sits.

53
00:03:50,841 --> 00:04:00,325
And if the fog is so thick that you cannot see a vehicle at a comfortable stopping distance, take the session to a closed track or the gym.

54
00:04:01,025 --> 00:04:07,015
When grip is scarce, skate the grippiest compound you own, not the fastest rolling one.

55
00:04:07,015 --> 00:04:09,907
Race wheels come in named firmness grades.

56
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:15,278
For most skaters, the winter choice is between an XFirm set and an XXFirm set.

57
00:04:15,278 --> 00:04:17,550
XFirm is the grippier of the two.

58
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:21,682
XXFirm is harder and tuned for roll and straight line speed.

59
00:04:21,682 --> 00:04:23,954
If you own both, skate the XFirm.

60
00:04:23,954 --> 00:04:29,462
If you have a softer Firm set from the wet season, that is the one to reach for.

61
00:04:29,462 --> 00:04:33,663
Do not raid your old, worn training set hoping for more grip.

62
00:04:33,663 --> 00:04:37,174
Worn, coned wheels give you less contact, not more.

63
00:04:37,174 --> 00:04:39,240
And be honest about the limit.

64
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:42,889
A grippier wheel does not make a dewy underpass safe.

65
00:04:42,889 --> 00:04:47,985
On those mornings, the answer is a slower first lap and a shorter session.

66
00:04:48,685 --> 00:04:51,215
Your body is stiffer in the cold too.

67
00:04:51,215 --> 00:04:58,326
The push in inline speed skating loads the groin, hip flexors and lower back through a long, deep range.

68
00:04:58,326 --> 00:05:03,932
A stiff body asked for full power in the first five minutes is how strains happen.

69
00:05:03,932 --> 00:05:07,214
So keep the same warm up, but roughly double it.

70
00:05:07,214 --> 00:05:13,915
Stage one is off the skates: easy movement, mobility and activation, for twelve to twenty minutes.

71
00:05:13,915 --> 00:05:18,838
Stage two is ten minutes of easy rolling with short strokes and no lean.

72
00:05:18,838 --> 00:05:22,736
Stage three builds a deeper position and tests your grip.

73
00:05:22,736 --> 00:05:25,812
Only then comes stage four, the hard efforts.

74
00:05:25,812 --> 00:05:29,231
Cold aches that ease once you are warm are normal.

75
00:05:29,231 --> 00:05:39,487
But sharp pain, swelling, numbness, a joint that gives way, or pain that lasts more than a few days is worth seeing a physiotherapist or doctor about.

76
00:05:40,187 --> 00:05:43,185
A race suit is built for air, not for warmth.

77
00:05:43,185 --> 00:05:47,381
A close, aerodynamic cut is exactly what you want at race pace.

78
00:05:47,381 --> 00:05:51,643
It is not what you want on a cold start line for twenty minutes.

79
00:05:51,643 --> 00:05:59,303
So warm up in a tracksuit or a thermal layer over your suit, and strip down shortly before the effort that matters.

80
00:05:59,303 --> 00:06:08,960
Cover your hands and your ears, because your fingers lose dexterity quickly in the cold, and you want a full grip if you have to put a hand down.

81
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:12,491
After the session, do not leave a damp suit in a bag.

82
00:06:12,491 --> 00:06:15,687
Wash it gently and dry it away from direct heat.

83
00:06:16,387 --> 00:06:19,145
Here is the part that catches people out.

84
00:06:19,145 --> 00:06:25,735
Cold metal brought into a warm, humid room collects condensation, just like a cold glass of water.

85
00:06:25,735 --> 00:06:29,837
That moisture lands on your bearing shields, axles and frame.

86
00:06:29,837 --> 00:06:34,544
If your skates go straight into a zipped bag, it sits there for hours.

87
00:06:34,544 --> 00:06:37,166
So treat a cold morning like a wet one.

88
00:06:37,166 --> 00:06:45,169
Wipe the wheels and the outside of the bearings, loosen the boots, and leave the skates where air can move around them.

89
00:06:45,169 --> 00:06:49,136
A shaded balcony or a shelf under a ceiling fan works well.

90
00:06:49,136 --> 00:06:54,180
Once everything is at room temperature, spin each wheel by hand and listen.

91
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:58,349
A short or gritty roll means it is time to clean, dry and oil.

92
00:06:59,049 --> 00:07:00,608
Here is your checklist.

93
00:07:00,608 --> 00:07:08,672
Check the forecast for fog, not just the temperature, because visibility decides whether a road session happens at all.

94
00:07:08,672 --> 00:07:14,636
Scout the first lap slowly, and note where the shade, paint, tiles and metal covers sit.

95
00:07:14,636 --> 00:07:20,938
Skate the grippiest compound you own, and keep your newest, roundest set for cold, damp days.

96
00:07:20,938 --> 00:07:28,325
Double your usual warm up, start it off the skates, and give the first ten minutes on wheels to easy rolling.

97
00:07:28,325 --> 00:07:30,629
Warm up in a layer over your suit.

98
00:07:30,629 --> 00:07:36,661
And afterwards, wipe down and air dry your skates, because condensation does the rusting.

99
00:07:37,361 --> 00:07:39,960
If you remember one thing, make it this.

100
00:07:39,960 --> 00:07:45,288
On a cold morning, your wheels, your oil and your body all start the session cold.

101
00:07:45,288 --> 00:07:50,615
Give all three time to warm up, and save your hardest efforts for the second half.

102
00:07:50,615 --> 00:07:56,983
The full guide, with the wheel grades and the whole cold weather routine, is on the LiveLica blog.

103
00:07:56,983 --> 00:07:58,997
The link is in the description.
